ches:** NautilusTrader is an open-source event-driven framework with a Rust core and Python API, multi-asset support out of the box (equities, futures, FX, crypto), and a trade-level simulation that handles partial fills, slippage models, and cross-asset correlation correctly. For intraday crypto/FX with order-book behavior, NautilusTrader is the choice with no serious alternative.  **Backtrader Status:** Backtrader remains a classic learning tool, but in 2026 it carries long-term maintenance risk, scaling limits, and dependency drift. It can be fine for small experiments, but it's rarely the optimal foundation for new work.  ## Use-Case Recommendations  If your strategy is fundamentally predictive, PyBroker is one of the most pragmatic frameworks for walkforward validation, training/test discipline, and ML-shaped backtesting workflows.  For an operational crypto bot framework with built-in backtesting, optimization, and live execution, Freqtrade is the most deployable open-source choice.  For daily-bar equity factor research, zipline-reloaded is recommended for Pipeline, and vectorbt for speed.  ## Core Trade-Off  Choosing among Python backtesting frameworks is mostly a choice between two execution models — vectorized and event-driven — and a set of trade-offs around speed, fidelity, and accounting correctness.   REMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks.

